PART 8: Amazon Route 53 & DNS

Interview Importance: ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ (Highest Priority)

DNS is one of the most frequently asked networking topics in AWS DevOps interviews. Many interviewers start with:

"What happens when you type google.com into a browser?"
"How does Route 53 work?"
"Difference between CNAME and Alias?"
"How does failover routing work?"

If you understand DNS well, many AWS networking concepts become much easier.

What is DNS?

DNS (Domain Name System) translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses.

Example:

Instead of remembering:

142.250.183.110

We type:

www.google.com

DNS converts:

www.google.com

↓

142.250.xxx.xxx

Think of DNS as the phonebook of the Internet.

What is Amazon Route 53?

Amazon Route 53 is AWS's:

DNS Service
Domain Registration Service
Health Checking Service
Traffic Routing Service

It routes users to AWS resources such as:

EC2
ALB
CloudFront
S3
API Gateway
Elastic Beanstalk
Why is it called Route 53?

Because DNS primarily uses Port 53.

Protocols:

UDP 53

TCP 53
